# .spool/project/project.req

feature: PROJECT

requirements:

  - id: PROJECT-1
    title: Absolute Project Path Required
    description: >
      Tool calls must accept the project argument as an absolute
      filesystem path; relative paths must be rejected with the
      invalid_project error category defined in SERVER-3.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-04
    rationale: >
      The MCP server's working directory is unrelated to the
      agent's working directory, so a relative path has no
      reliable anchor. Requiring absolute paths makes the
      contract unambiguous and forces the calling agent to
      resolve the path itself, where it has the context to
      do so correctly.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: PROJECT-1-1
        title: Absolute Path Accepted
        description: >
          Given a tool call with an absolute path that resolves
          to a directory containing a valid .spool.yaml,
          when the tool is invoked,
          then the tool runs the Spool pipeline against that
          project and returns a successful result.

      - id: PROJECT-1-2
        title: Relative Path Rejected
        description: >
          Given a tool call with a relative path,
          when the tool is invoked,
          then the response is an error with category
          invalid_project and a message identifying that the
          path must be absolute.

  - id: PROJECT-2
    title: Fresh Index Per Call
    description: >
      Every tool call must rebuild the traceability index from
      the project's current on-disk contents; no state may carry
      across calls.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-04
    rationale: >
      Caching across calls would require invalidation logic
      tied to filesystem mtimes or file watchers, neither of
      which are robust across agent edit patterns or git
      operations. Rebuilding every call keeps the server
      stateless, matches Spool's own model, and removes a class
      of staleness bugs entirely. If profiling later shows that
      coverage runs are a measurable cost, caching can be
      revisited with concrete numbers in hand.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: PROJECT-2-1
        title: Req File Change Reflected
        description: >
          Given a tool call followed by a modification to a
          .req file in the project,
          when a subsequent tool call is made,
          then the result reflects the modified contents.

      - id: PROJECT-2-2
        title: New Test File Reflected
        description: >
          Given a tool call followed by the addition of a new
          test file containing a Spool annotation,
          when a subsequent tool call is made,
          then the new annotation appears in the result.

      - id: PROJECT-2-3
        title: Deleted Requirement Reflected
        description: >
          Given a tool call followed by deletion of a .req file,
          when a subsequent tool call is made,
          then no requirement or AC from the deleted file
          appears in the result.

  - id: PROJECT-3
    title: Project Loading Diagnostics
    description: >
      Non-fatal parse and scan warnings produced while loading
      a project must be returned alongside the tool's result; a
      malformed .spool.yaml is fatal and returns a dedicated
      error category.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-04
    rationale: >
      Spool already distinguishes between fatal pipeline failure
      (the project cannot be loaded at all) and per-file warnings
      (a single .req or test file could not be processed but the
      rest of the pipeline ran). Carrying that distinction
      through to the MCP response lets agents act on partial
      results rather than treating any diagnostic as a hard
      failure.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: PROJECT-3-1
        title: Parse Warnings Surfaced
        description: >
          Given a project containing one malformed .req file
          and other valid .req files,
          when a tool is invoked,
          then the response contains a successful result drawn
          from the valid files together with a warning entry
          identifying the malformed file.

      - id: PROJECT-3-2
        title: Scan Warnings Surfaced
        description: >
          Given a project where one matched test file cannot
          be read,
          when a tool is invoked,
          then the response contains a successful result drawn
          from the readable files together with a warning entry
          identifying the unreadable file.

      - id: PROJECT-3-3
        title: Invalid Config Is Fatal
        description: >
          Given a project whose .spool.yaml exists but is not
          valid YAML or is missing required fields,
          when a tool is invoked,
          then the response is an error with category
          invalid_config and a message describing the problem.
